Dear Sir or Madam,

I write to place before the Lords Justice and Home Affairs Committee a docu=
mentary record raising a systemic question within the Committee's remit on t=
he administration of justice: the access of disabled litigants in person to =
the official transcripts of proceedings affecting them, where those transcri=
pts are required to support a registered criminal investigation, an active j=
udicial review, and ongoing Chancery enforcement.

This is my first written-evidence submission to this Committee. The Commons=
 Justice Committee has previously received eleven written-evidence submissio=
ns across 78 days, none of which has produced a substantive response, and is=
 therefore not the addressee of this submission. The Liaison Committee is co=
pied for oversight purposes.

I am Nadia Zahmoul, a litigant in person across six live High Court proceed=
ings. I am autistic (NHS diagnosis, Central London Adult Autism Service, Jul=
y 2025). I have PTSD, chronic since 2013, and Sj=C3=B6gren's Syndrome, biopsy-co=
nfirmed October 2025, in active flare. I have =C2=A385 in my bank account. I hav=
e no income. I have no permanent home. I have over =C2=A31 million in liability =
against me, accruing daily, anchored in characterisations of my documented d=
isabilities as moral failing.

The matters identified in this submission concern the treatment of evidence=
 on the face of a judicial record, rather than the merits of any underlying =
decision. The question is not one of appellate review, but of whether the ha=
ndling of that evidence engages systemic issues beyond the ordinary supervis=
ory function of the courts.

The systemic question

By what mechanism, and on what authority, can a disabled litigant in person=
 be denied access at public expense to the official transcripts of the proce=
edings against her, where those transcripts are required to support: (a) a r=
egistered criminal investigation operationally engaged by the Metropolitan P=
olice Service (CCR-6501-26-0100-000); (b) an active judicial review (AC-2025=
-LON-004293) currently in the permission stage; and (c) ongoing Chancery enf=
orcement of liability anchored in the very findings the transcripts would te=
st?

The documentary record

On 27 March 2026, I filed Form EX107 with HMCTS Family Division applying fo=
r the official transcript of the ten-day final hearing in BV20D01752 (19 to =
28 February 2024) at public expense. The application was refused by the tria=
l judge on 27 March 2026 in a one-line refusal, and again on 30 March 2026 i=
n a structured refusal. I formally requested written reasons on 30 March 202=
6, and again on 14 April 2026. As at the date of this submission, 31 days ha=
ve elapsed since the original request. No written reasons have been provided=
.

I now file with this Committee, as written evidence, seven Form EX107 appli=
cations, each for a single hearing day:
EX107 =E2=80=94 20 February 2024 =E2=80=94 Trial Day 1
EX107 =E2=80=94 21 February 2024 =E2=80=94 Trial Day 2
EX107 =E2=80=94 22 February 2024 =E2=80=94 Trial Day 3
EX107 =E2=80=94 23 February 2024 =E2=80=94 Trial Day 4
EX107 =E2=80=94 26 February 2024 =E2=80=94 Trial Day 5
EX107 =E2=80=94 28 February 2024 =E2=80=94 Trial Day 6
EX107 =E2=80=94 8 May 2025 =E2=80=94 Hearing
The seven applications are filed as discrete applications, not as a single =
application for the entirety of a ten-day hearing. The trial judge's stated =
objection on 30 March 2026 =E2=80=94 that "transcribing the entirety of a ten-day =
hearing represents a very considerable call on public funds" =E2=80=94 does not ar=
ise on a per-day application.

The full email chain on which the trial judge's structured refusal of 30 Ma=
rch 2026 sits is attached, so the Committee can see the original application=
, the four grounds advanced, the structured refusal, and my point-by-point r=
eply of 31 March 2026.

A covering letter accompanies the seven applications, addressing the four g=
rounds for public expense and the procedural matters raised in the refusal o=
f 30 March 2026.

What I am placing before the Committee

The Committee is not asked to determine the underlying applications. The ap=
plications are placed on the parliamentary record as evidence of how the acc=
ess-to-transcripts framework operates in practice for a disabled litigant in=
 person, where every formal channel of remedy has been engaged and none has =
produced an answer.

The matters set out in this submission, and the documents attached, are pla=
ced before the Committee for such use as the Committee considers appropriate=
 to its remit on the administration of justice.

Yours faithfully,

Nadia Zahmoul

Litigant in Person

